  \left\{
  \begin{array}{ll}
  \Delta^2u-\beta\Delta u=\lambda u+\left(\frac{1}{|x|^\mu}*F(u)\right)f(u),
  \quad\mbox{in}\ \ \mathbb{R}^4,
  \displaystyle\int_{\mathbb{R}^4}|u|^2dx=c^2,\quad u\in H^2(\mathbb{R}^4),
  \end{array}
  \right.
  \end{split}
  \end{align*} under specific constraints and growth conditions for $\beta$, $\lambda$, and $f$, and we leverage a homotopy-based minimax principle to establish the existence of a standardized ground state solution for this equation. Notably, we consider an exponential critical growth term and a nonautonomous term in $u$, as well as a critical Hardy-Sobolev type term with expansion rates and we demonstrate the standardization condition can be fulfilled by employing a new compactness lemma. This achieves our goal of investigating the standardized ground state solution for this type of equation with critical exponential growth and we believe our approach to handling the standardization condition will be useful in future research. Additionally, an application for a rotating Bose-Einstein condensates can be found in the appendix. We also demonstrate the result we obtain here can be used to prove the standardization condition for an equation with a weakly coupled term. This means if we remove the standardization condition, we can prove the existence of a ground state solution for a biharmonic equation with critical exponential growth and we outline the modifications to the proof in the appendix. This achieves our goal of investigating the ground state solution for this type of equation with critical exponential growth and we believe our approach will be useful in future research on Bose-Einstein condensates. This allows us to use a compactness lemma to show that an equation with critical exponential growth and a nonautonomous term in $u$ as well as a critical Hardy-Sobolev type term with expansion rates has a ground state solution and we believe our approach will be useful in future research on Bose-Einstein condensates. This allows us to use a compactness lemma to show that an equation with critical exponential growth and a nonautonomous term in

